Below are three simple examples—pick the one that best fits your environment—to “turn off” (deny) access to your own site or service. Use responsibly; these are meant for maintenance/administration, not malicious purposes.

1) Node.js / Express middleware  
```js
// deny-access.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Example: deny ALL requests
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.status(503).send('Service Unavailable – access denied.');
});

// If you wanted to whitelist only certain IPs, you could do:
// const whitelist = new Set(['203.0.113.5', '198.51.100.7']);
// app.use((req, res, next) => {
//   if (!whitelist.has(req.ip)) {
//     return res.status(403).send('Access Denied');
//   }
//   next();
// });

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  // This will never be reached in “deny all” mode
  res.send('Hello, world!');
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Listening on ${PORT}, access is DENIED.`));
```
